sections.body:
A **functional interface** is an interface that declares exactly one **abstract** method. It's allowed to also have any number of `default` and `static` methods (which already have bodies and aren't 'missing' an implementation), but only one method total may be left unimplemented for something else to fill in.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Greeter {
    String greet(String name);   // the ONE abstract method

    default Greeter shouting() {
        return name -> greet(name).toUpperCase();   // default method -- doesn't count against the limit
    }
}
```

`@FunctionalInterface` is an optional annotation -- it doesn't change how the interface behaves, but it tells the compiler to actually verify the one-abstract-method shape and raise a compile error if you accidentally add a second one, which is a cheap, worthwhile safeguard on any interface you intend people to implement with a lambda. `Runnable` (one method, `run()`), and `Comparator<T>` (one abstract method, `compare(T, T)`, despite also having several default methods like `reversed()` and `thenComparing()`) are both functional interfaces from the standard library, and both predate lambdas entirely -- lambdas didn't require inventing new interfaces, they just gave a much shorter way to implement existing single-method ones. The 'exactly one abstract method' rule is not incidental: a lambda's body is just an expression or block of statements with no method name attached to it at all, so the compiler needs exactly one unambiguous slot -- one method signature -- to know what that body is actually implementing. Two abstract methods would leave the question 'which one does this lambda's body correspond to?' with no good answer.

When a lambda's entire body is nothing more than calling one already-existing method, a **method reference** (`::`) says the same thing more directly, without introducing a parameter name that's never actually used for anything but forwarding. There are four distinct forms:

```java
// 1. Static method reference
Function<String, Integer> parse = Integer::parseInt;          // same as: s -> Integer.parseInt(s)

// 2. Instance method reference on a PARTICULAR, already-existing object
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
Consumer<String> adder = names::add;                          // same as: s -> names.add(s)

// 3. Instance method reference on an ARBITRARY object of a given type
Function<String, Integer> len = String::length;                // same as: s -> s.length()
// here, the lambda's own parameter becomes the object the method is called ON

// 4. Constructor reference
Supplier<List<String>> factory = ArrayList::new;               // same as: () -> new ArrayList<>()
```

The third form is the one most likely to look confusing at first, because `String::length` doesn't visually show any object being called on -- but that's exactly the point: when used as a `Function<String, Integer>`, the single incoming parameter itself becomes the receiver that `.length()` is called on. All four forms are pure shorthand; anywhere you can write a method reference, you could instead write out the equivalent lambda by hand -- method references exist purely to remove ceremony when a lambda would add nothing beyond forwarding to one existing method.

A lambda body can freely *read* local variables and parameters from its enclosing scope. But there's a firm restriction on which ones: a captured local variable must be **effectively final** -- meaning it is never reassigned anywhere in its scope after its initial assignment, even if it isn't explicitly declared `final`.

```java
int total = 0;
Runnable r = () -> {
    total++;                      // does NOT compile: total is reassigned, so it's not effectively final
};
```

This restriction exists because a lambda can genuinely outlive the method call that created it -- it might be stored in a field, passed off to another thread, or invoked much later -- and Java sidesteps an entire category of 'which value did it actually capture, and when' ambiguity by simply disallowing capture of anything that changes. When you genuinely need a lambda to accumulate shared, mutable state, the standard workaround is to capture a reference to a mutable *container* instead of a primitive -- a one-element array, or (more idiomatically, especially across threads) an `AtomicInteger` or `AtomicReference` -- since the *reference itself* stays effectively final even while the container's contents change.

Separately, `this` inside a lambda behaves differently from `this` inside an old-style anonymous inner class, and it's worth being precise about why. A lambda does not introduce its own instance or its own `this` at all -- `this` inside a lambda body refers to whatever `this` would mean at that exact point in the enclosing code, exactly as if the lambda's body had been written directly inline. An anonymous inner class, by contrast, genuinely creates its own object with its own `this`, so a method call inside it that's meant to reach the enclosing instance requires the more verbose `EnclosingClass.this.method()`. This is a real, practical advantage of lambdas over anonymous classes whenever the callback body needs to call back into the surrounding object.

