sections.body:
Intermediate operations don't process any elements at all when they're called -- they just record a step in the pipeline's description. Nothing actually happens until a terminal operation is invoked, at which point elements are pulled through the entire chain, generally one at a time, from source to terminal.

```java
List<String> names = List.of("amy", "ben", "cara");

names.stream()
    .map(n -> {
        System.out.println("mapping " + n);
        return n.toUpperCase();
    });
// prints NOTHING -- there's no terminal operation, so this entire pipeline never actually runs

long count = names.stream()
    .map(n -> {
        System.out.println("mapping " + n);
        return n.toUpperCase();
    })
    .count();
// NOW it prints "mapping amy", "mapping ben", "mapping cara" -- count() is a terminal operation
```

This laziness is not just an implementation curiosity -- it's what allows Java to apply real optimizations, like stopping early for `findFirst()` or `anyMatch()` without ever processing the rest of a large or even infinite source, since the pipeline only pulls as many elements through as the terminal operation actually needs. The practical consequence worth remembering is the opposite direction: a chain of intermediate operations with no terminal operation attached at all compiles perfectly fine and does absolutely nothing at run time -- a silent no-op that's easy to leave behind by accident if you forget the final `.collect(...)` or `.forEach(...)`.

`collect(Collector)` is the general-purpose terminal operation for gathering a stream's elements into some concrete structure. The `Collectors` utility class supplies ready-made collectors for the overwhelming majority of cases: `Collectors.toList()` / `toSet()` for the obvious cases, `Collectors.toMap(keyFn, valueFn)` for building a `Map`, `Collectors.joining(delimiter)` for concatenating a stream of `String`s, and `Collectors.groupingBy(classifierFn)` for partitioning elements into a `Map<K, List<T>>` keyed by whatever the classifier function computes.

```java
List<String> words = List.of("pear", "fig", "kiwi", "plum", "date");

Map<Integer, List<String>> byLength = words.stream()
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy(String::length));
// {3=[fig], 4=[pear, kiwi, date], 5=... wait -- "plum" has length 4 too}
// actual result: {3=[fig], 4=[pear, kiwi, plum, date]}

String joined = words.stream()
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.joining(", "));
System.out.println(joined);   // pear, fig, kiwi, plum, date
```

One last, sharp-edged rule ties this whole concept together: a `Stream` can only be traversed **once**. Calling a terminal operation consumes the stream; any further operation attempted on that same stream reference afterward -- another `collect`, another `forEach`, anything -- throws `IllegalStateException: stream has already been operated upon or closed`. If you need to run the same or a different pipeline again, you don't reuse the old stream -- you call `.stream()` again on the original underlying source to get a brand-new one.

