`Optional.of(value)` wraps a value that must not be `null` (throwing `NullPointerException` immediately if it is); `Optional.ofNullable(value)` is the safer choice when the value genuinely might be `null`, producing an empty `Optional` in that case instead of throwing; `Optional.empty()` explicitly represents 'nothing here.'

`get()` throws `NoSuchElementException` if the `Optional` is empty -- calling it unconditionally, without checking presence first, reproduces exactly the bug `Optional` exists to prevent, just under a different exception name. The idiomatic alternatives almost always read better and are safer by construction:

```java
Optional<User> maybeUser = findUserById(42);

String name = maybeUser.map(User::getName).orElse("Unknown");

User user = maybeUser.orElseThrow(() -> new NoSuchElementException("No user with id 42"));

maybeUser.ifPresent(u -> System.out.println("Found: " + u.getName()));
```

`orElse(defaultValue)` and `orElseGet(supplier)` look similar but behave differently in one important way: `orElse`'s argument is evaluated *eagerly*, every time, even when the `Optional` already has a value and the default will simply be discarded; `orElseGet`'s supplier is only *ever invoked if the Optional is actually empty*. If computing the default is cheap (a literal, an existing object), the difference is invisible; if it's expensive (a database query, a fresh allocation), using `orElse` there quietly does that expensive work on every single call, present or not -- `orElseGet` is the correct choice whenever the fallback costs anything non-trivial to produce.

A **compact constructor** lets you add validation or normalization logic without repeating the full parameter list or the field assignments, which happen implicitly right after the compact constructor's own body finishes:

```java
record Range(int start, int end) {
    public Range {                       // no parameter list repeated -- it's implicit
        if (start > end) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("start must not exceed end");
        }
    }                                     // this.start = start; this.end = end; happens automatically after this
}
```

Records can also declare additional constructors (which must ultimately delegate to the canonical one) and additional instance or static methods beyond the generated accessors, exactly like a regular class.

There is one genuinely sharp edge worth knowing before it surprises you in real code: if a record component's type is an **array**, the automatically generated `equals()` and `hashCode()` compare that component by **reference identity**, not by content -- `Arrays.equals` is not used automatically, because the generated code simply calls `Object.equals` (and `Object.hashCode`) on each component, and arrays never override those from `Object`. A record like `record Buffer(byte[] data) { }` therefore does *not* behave as true value-equality for its `data` field -- two `Buffer` instances wrapping arrays with identical bytes are still not `.equals()` to each other, exactly the same pitfall that plain arrays already have outside of records entirely.

